# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Tooling caches
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Governor run output — regenerable, and evidence is retention-governed (AD-19, FR109)
workspace/

# BMAD skill renders — regenerated by render_skill.py
_bmad/render/

# Source research summarising internal decks and third-party internal proposals.
# Kept locally, deliberately not published. See README note.
doc/

# Run evidence IS published. The submission cites run seals and proof-card
# digests, and an integrity claim nobody outside this machine can check is not
# an integrity claim. Only the transient files are excluded: a lock is not an
# artifact, and a -wal/-shm pair committed mid-run yields a database missing the
# very events its seal covers.
runs/**/*.writer-lock
runs/**/*.db-wal
runs/**/*.db-shm

# The console's own output is the exception, and only because nothing cites it.
# These are made by clicking a card or running compare.py, regenerate in seconds,
# and appear in no submission, card or disclosure. runs/features IS published,
# because submission/outcomefuse-features.html quotes its seals.
runs/console/
runs/compare/
runs/compare-jobs/
runs/gallery/
runs/verify/

# Rendered media. Every frame of it is derived from the sealed logs this repo
# does publish, so excluding it costs no verifiability — a reader can re-render
# it — while committing it would put hundreds of megabytes of regenerable binary
# in a history nobody can prune.
*.mp4
*.webm
*.mov
*.mkv
*.wav
*.m4a

# The executive demo is rendered output plus the node toolchain that renders it.
# `render.cjs` rebuilds the whole thing from `storyboard.json` and a live
# console, and `artifact-hashes.json` is a digest of artifacts that are
# themselves not kept, so the folder proves nothing once its media is gone.
submission/executive-demo/
runs/video-tools/
